export const MDNA ={
  "Barco ClickShare":{
    why:"Barco's newest product is its most limited. The Teams-certified ClickShare Hub displays one source even on the dual-display Pro, takes 16 connections against 32 on the seven-year-old CX line, outputs at 30 Hz, is LAN-only, carries a 3-year warranty against CX's SmartCare 5, requires a Microsoft Teams Rooms license at your cost, and accepts nothing but a physical Button: no app, no AirPlay, no Cast, no Miracast. Barco's newest product has fewer ways to share than its oldest. Polaris answers with software: share from the browser on the device you already carry, share a whole workspace instead of one screen, and cross networks the Button can't even see. Barco's capability arrives with new hardware: Dual Sharing needs base firmware 2.20-plus and Desktop App 4.40-plus, and Wi-Fi 6E arrives only with Hub and a Gen 5 Button that works with no CX, C or Bar unit. Ours keeps the room improving on one install.",
    concede:"Honest concession: ClickShare's Button is an elegant guest experience, and Barco's enterprise brand carries weight in a boardroom RFP. If a customer's whole requirement is 'one presenter, one screen, zero training,' the Button is a fine answer — until the second presenter wants the screen too."},
  "Airtame":{
    why:"Airtame built a clean, simple product for sharing one screen in an SMB or classroom, and named us their number-one alternative for a reason. Their browser path is genuinely good and works even on an unlicensed device. The gate is elsewhere: on Airtame 3, Miracast is public beta, AirPlay is at best newly public beta, and Google Cast is listed as Planned with public beta targeted for September 2026, so a buyer replacing Airtame 2s loses production-grade native casting. And when the licence lapses, signage, scheduled content, emergency alerts, device alerts and all conferencing stop. Their update-pricing attack on Mersive is answered on our open update-policy page. Unlike theirs, our subscription carries the warranty for its whole life.",
    concede:"Credit where it's due: Airtame's openness culture (public roadmap, open docs, simple SKUs) is the best in the category, and their price point is real. For a signage-heavy estate with light meeting needs, they're a credible pick."},
  "ScreenBeam":{
    why:"ScreenBeam's app-free Miracast story is real, and it ends at the connection. Multi-View does four presenters. What ScreenBeam's own release notes add is that Multi-View is not available during a BYOM session, and that the Conference-mode second presenter was deprecated in January 2025: during a wireless conference, ScreenBeam is single-presenter. And 'no subscriptions, ever' has ended. Signage+, Message Manager, Alert+ and Active Learning now sit behind a 999.99 dollar Administrative Tools license that also requires ScreenBeam Cloud. Polaris treats the connection as the beginning of the meeting, not the whole product.",
    concede:"A concession up front: for Windows-heavy K-12 districts that want native Miracast at a low price with list pricing on the page, ScreenBeam is honest, cheap, and effective."},
  "WolfVision":{
    why:"Cynap is capable hardware at a price that usually surprises people, and its conferencing runs in-unit and is certified by neither Microsoft nor Zoom - neither vendor lists any Cynap model. On Cynap Pure (Next Gen), HDMI input, the browser and web conferencing all sit behind a paid Feature Pack, and dual-display support does not ship until Q3 2026. Polaris reaches the same rung with the vendors' own native clients. WolfVision publishes no current pricing on its own domain; the one costed configuration available is a 40-workstation MATRIX room - a Cynap Pro main station, a $2,540 feature pack and forty endpoints, roughly $70k-$140k at WolfVision's own 2022 MSRP list, before displays and labour. Their documentation is open and their engineering is respectable. WolfVision refreshed its entry and mid tiers in 2026 and left the ceiling exactly where it was: four on-screen windows, on every model, including the pre-announced Cynap Ultra. The still-current Cynap Pro and Core Pro remain Wi-Fi 5 and 1GigE.",
    concede:"Give them their due: WolfVision's visualizer heritage and education install base are real, and their open, model-organized support area is a pattern we admire enough to have copied."},
  "Kramer VIA":{
    why:"VIA is a wireless-collaboration line inside a signal-management company: six models with no comparison table between them, Wi-Fi 5 across the entire line including the brand-new Connect 3, no PoE on any model, 4K capped at 30 Hz everywhere, and Campus&sup2; still shipping Windows 10, where Kramer's own product copy says running Teams requires an upgrade to a Windows 11 license. In July 2026 Kramer excluded VIA from its own price-stability commitment, citing processor, memory and OS-licensing volatility. Polaris is one platform with one management plane and security documentation an IT evaluator can actually read.",
    concede:"To be fair: Kramer's broader AV ecosystem, public tiered partner program, and integrator enablement are strong. If the deal is a whole-building signal path, they're in it."},
  "Crestron AirMedia":{
    why:"AirMedia is a module of the Crestron ecosystem: a capable share receiver designed to pull the room into a larger Crestron stack, quoted by a dealer and explained by an integrator. AirMedia Canvas does composite four sources, automatically, with no layout control, with Swap Sources disabled above two sources, and with one of the two factory system modes silently pinning the limit to one. Overflow disconnects AirPlay and Miracast users rather than queueing them. There is no browser join, no cross-network path, no remote-participant story of any kind, and no secure-boot or root-of-trust claim in any Crestron AirMedia document. Polaris is the whole collaboration platform on one device, quoted and configured without a dealer in the loop.",
    concede:"No argument: Crestron's docs-on-page discipline, certificate transparency, and dealer machinery are the best in AV. If the project is a whole-building control stack, AirMedia rides along at a price that's hard to argue with."},
  "Vivi":{
    why:"Vivi built an education engagement suite with casting attached, and competes hardest on the classroom-engagement rows. Fair fight on those rows; the platform underneath is thinner: app-centric sharing on modest hardware, no true BYOM bridging, no room-hosted meetings, no cross-network path, and a channel model that keeps US routing in-house. Polaris counters in the classroom with web-join on Chromebooks, native Google Cast, and Route and Engage, on silicon built for an 10-year life.",
    concede:"Worth conceding: Vivi has built suite offerings we simply don't have, including PA and bell-system integration, campus-wide emergency alerts and announcements, and a school-communications layer alongside the classroom tools. A district buying an all-in-one campus communications platform has real reasons to shortlist them, and their proof density (every logo links to a story) backs it up. Where we win is the layer underneath: the sharing, the silicon, and the 10-year platform the classroom runs on."},
  "MTR / Zoom Rooms":{
    why:"MTR and Zoom Rooms solved 'walk in and it works', and charged an ecosystem for it: a per-room, per-year license, one platform provisioned per room, and a platform change that means re-provisioning or replacing the room. Worse is what they do to the room itself: a certified room is designed around one path to the display, the meeting, so co-located teams start a call to share a file and every head drops into a laptop. The market has priced that in &mdash; Barco now sells ClickShare Hub specifically to add wireless presenting to a Teams Room, at a second hardware purchase, a second licence and, per Barco's own known-issues page, only wireless presenting, with conferencing capability not yet supported at this moment. Polaris reaches the same summit (the room hosts, native clients, no user device) while keeping platform choice per meeting and letting people share to the workspace without a call at all. Same capability, no lock, no license stack, heads up.",
    concede:"Honest concession: certified rooms are polished, deeply integrated, and backed by the platform vendors themselves. If an organization is single-platform forever by policy, the lock-in cost is invisible to them — until the policy changes."},
  _all:{
    why:"Read the matrix by group and a pattern appears: every competitor wins somewhere on the bottom rung (a cheaper share, a simpler button, a native cast). Of the twelve competitors we checked in August 2026, none holds the full agnostic column on one device and one install: a multi-share workspace with no fixed ceiling, true wireless BYOM, cross-network sharing, room-hosted meetings with native clients, on Linux-hardened silicon with the security story in writing. Individual rungs are reachable &mdash; Barco with Conference, Airtame with Hybrid, Cisco with native clients &mdash; and each gives something up to get there. That column is the product. The rungs below it are features.",
    concede:"All claims pending canonical-matrix baseline and legal review (Q6 committee). Concession style follows the Airtame pattern deliberately: credibility is a feature."}
};

